{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ואהבת לרעך כמוך", :translit "v'ahavta l're'akha kamokha", :gloss "and you shall love your fellow as yourself"} {:he "לא תרביע כלאים", :translit "lo tarbi'a kil'aim", :gloss "you shall not breed mixed species"} {:he "האוהב אשתו", :translit "ha'ohev ishto", :gloss "one who loves his wife"} {:he "ולא יכפנה לתשמיש", :translit "v'lo yechafeneh l'tashmish", :gloss "and must not feign for relations"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut: juxtaposition of adjacent parashiyah topics yields the lesson — love of one's wife paired with prohibition of kil'aim warns against mental adultery"}], :frame {:text "Rabbi Akiva declares 'You shall love your fellow as yourself' (Leviticus 19:18) a klal gadol ba-Torah, a great generative principle of the Torah (Sifra, Kedoshim 4:12; Yerushalmi Nedarim 9:4). The prohibition of kil'aim in Leviticus 19:19 is juxtaposed immediately after this verse. Ba'al HaTurim reads the juxtaposition as a warning against introducing a foreign image into the intimate act — relations with one's wife while fantasizing about another woman constitutes a form of kil'aim in the marital bond.", :sources ["Leviticus 19:18" "Leviticus 19:19" "Sifra Kedoshim 4:12" "Yerushalmi Nedarim 9:4"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut: juxtaposition of adjacent parashiyah topics yields the lesson — love of one's wife paired with prohibition of kil'aim warns against mental adultery", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:24:19.342108919Z", :model "glm-5.2"}